course: dictionary
lesson: 10912
title: Building your vocabulary
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your vocabulary is all the words you know and use. A wide vocabulary means you know lots of words and can choose the right one for what you want to say. You build it by reading, listening and learning new words, one at a time.
explain: Vocabulary can also mean the special words of a subject. In maths, words like sum, difference, product and quotient are maths vocabulary. Every subject has its own words to learn.
explain: «Audiobooks build vocabulary, understanding and a love of stories, but they do not practise reading printed words, so children use both.» Listening and reading each help in a different way, so using both is a good idea.
fact: Vocabulary means all the words a person knows or uses.
fact: The vocabulary of a subject is the special words used in it, such as maths vocabulary.
fact: A vocabulary list is a list of words with their meanings, often used to learn a foreign language.
fact: Reading widely helps your vocabulary grow.
fact: Sum, difference, product and quotient are all words from maths vocabulary.
exercise: What is a person's vocabulary? | Only the words written on a page | *All the words they know or use | The number of books they own | Their favourite story
exercise: Which word is maths vocabulary? | Happy | *Quotient | Garden | Football
exercise: «Audiobooks build vocabulary, understanding and a love of stories.» What can audiobooks help you do? | *Build vocabulary and understanding | Replace every printed book | Stop learning new words | Forget every story you hear
test: What helps your vocabulary grow most? | Waiting for others to tell you the words | Only watching films | *Reading widely | Never opening a book
test: What is a vocabulary list? | A list of your friends | A shopping list only | *A list of words with their meanings, used for learning | A list of numbers in a sum
exam: Why might a child use both audiobooks and printed books? | Because printed books are never useful | Because audiobooks replace all reading | Because audiobooks are only for maths | *Because audiobooks do not practise reading printed words, so both help
